    "perplexity": {
      "description": "London 0 Hull 4 is The Housemartins’ bright, sharply written 1986 debut, a defining example of British indie pop built on jangling guitars, brisk rhythms, melodic harmonies, and an unmistakable northern character. The album balances infectious songs with pointed social observation: “Happy Hour” turns workplace sexism into a buoyant pop anthem, while “Sheep,” “Sitting on a Fence,” and “Get Up Off Our Knees” challenge apathy, conformity, and political disengagement. Its football-score title nods to the band’s hometown of Kingston upon Hull.\n\nThe record’s concise twelve-track original LP helped establish Paul Heaton and Norman Cook as major songwriting and musical talents, while singles including “Happy Hour,” “Think for a Minute,” “Flag Day,” and “Sheep” brought the group a UK Top 10 album and platinum-level success. Still admired for its wit, immediacy, and melodic craft, London 0 Hull 4 remains an important bridge between 1980s independent pop and the Britpop generation that followed. This 40th-anniversary edition presents the original album on limited mint-green vinyl, issued by Go! Discs and UMC.",
